WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 286: George Washington Carver: The Peanut Wizard, the Jesup Wagon, and Agricultural Rebirth

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.680
[Rebecca] Welcome to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.680 --> 00:00:06.880
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:06.880 --> 00:00:19.520
[Rebecca] Today, our top 50 famous non-president American series celebrates the saintly agricultural pioneer whose scientific genius and love for the natural world healed the soil and people of the South.

4
00:00:19.520 --> 00:00:26.640
[Rebecca] Here is George with the extraordinary life of George Washington Carver.

5
00:00:26.640 --> 00:00:40.080
[George] Born into slavery on Moses Carver's farm near Diamond Grove, Missouri, in the final years of the Civil War, young George and his mother Mary were kidnapped by Confederate bushwhackers when he was barely a few weeks old.

6
00:00:40.080 --> 00:00:46.640
[George] Moses Carver traded his prized racehorse to ransom the infant, though his mother was never seen again.

7
00:00:46.640 --> 00:01:05.1000
[George] Raised by Moses and Susan Carver after emancipation, frail, asthmatic George was spared heavy farm labor, spending his days exploring the woods, tending secret gardens of wildflowers, and earning the neighborhood nickname, the plant doctor for his uncanny ability to revive sick house plants.

8
00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:20.720
[George] Barred from Highland College in Kansas when administrators discovered he was black, Carver homesteaded a sawed house in Kansas before enrolling at Simpson College in Iowa to study art, recognizing his extraordinary botanical intuition.

9
00:01:20.720 --> 00:01:35.360
[George] His art teacher urged him to transfer to Iowa State Agricultural College in Ames, where Carver became the first black student, earning his master's degree in bacterial botany and winning acclaim as a brilliant mycologist and plant pathologist.

10
00:01:35.360 --> 00:01:54.480
[George] Growing up on the isolated Missouri farm, young George's frailty and recurring respiratory sickness spared him the grueling physical labor of the fields, allowing him to spend blissful hours roaming the woodlands, collecting unusual mosses, and cataloging wildflower varieties in his handmade sketchbooks.

11
00:01:54.480 --> 00:02:09.080
[George] The local farm wives, noticing that the sickly young black boy could magically nurse wilting ferns and diseased tomato plants back to vibrant health, began bringing their sick plants to him for diagnosis, christening him the plant doctor.

12
00:02:09.080 --> 00:02:26.120
[George] When he was denied admission to Highland College in Kansas purely because of the color of his skin, Carver refused to succumb to bitterness, homesteading a claim on the Kansas Prairie where he built a sod house, cooked over buffalo chips, and continued his botanical studies in solitude.

13
00:02:26.120 --> 00:02:28.600
[George] In 1896, Booker T.

14
00:02:28.600 --> 00:02:37.000
[George] Washington, founder of the Tuskegee Normal and Industrial Institute in Alabama, sent a famous letter summoning Carver to the Deep South.

15
00:02:37.000 --> 00:02:40.200
[George] I cannot offer you money, position or fame.

16
00:02:40.200 --> 00:02:44.520
[George] The first two you have, the last from the place you probably will get.

17
00:02:44.520 --> 00:02:48.920
[George] We are looking for something that will help our people, something to lift them up.

18
00:02:48.920 --> 00:02:57.320
[George] Carver accepted immediately, leaving a comfortable research career in Iowa for the impoverished pine hills of Macon County, Alabama.

19
00:02:57.320 --> 00:03:03.080
[George] Arriving at Tuskegee, Carver found no laboratory, no beakers and no funding.

20
00:03:03.080 --> 00:03:15.000
[George] Undeterred, Carver and his students scavenged trash heaps, turning empty tin cans into bunts and burners, broken bottles into test tubes, and discarded kitchen cups into mortars and pestles.

21
00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:22.680
[George] For the next forty-seven years, this improvised laboratory would serve as the epicenter of a Southern agricultural revolution.

22
00:03:22.680 --> 00:03:23.800
[George] Booker T.

23
00:03:24.040 --> 00:03:39.720
[George] Washington's historic telegram arrived at Iowa State just as Carver was completing his master's degree and being offered prestigious faculty positions, presenting him with a choice between personal professional comfort and sacrificial service to his oppressed people.

24
00:03:39.720 --> 00:03:53.760
[George] Arriving in rural Alabama in 1896, Carver was shocked by the abject squalor and generational malnutrition of the black sharecroppers, whose shacks lacked floors, clean drinking water, and basic sanitation.

25
00:03:53.760 --> 00:04:00.080
[George] Carver's first makeshift laboratory at Tuskegee was an inspiring triumph of improvisational genius.

26
00:04:00.080 --> 00:04:16.960
[George] He sent his students into school dumpsters to salvage broken bottles, zinc jar lids, and discarded saucepans, demonstrating to his pupils that true scientific inquiry required resourcefulness, curiosity, and moral purpose rather than expensive equipment.

27
00:04:16.960 --> 00:04:22.240
[George] Carson observed that a century of monoculture cotton farming had devastated the South.

28
00:04:22.240 --> 00:04:33.040
[George] Cotton stripped the soil of essential nitrogen, leaving red clay fields eroded, barren, and exhausted, while sharecroppers were trapped in permanent debt to white landowners.

29
00:04:33.040 --> 00:04:36.800
[George] Carver recognized that the soil was starving for nutrients.

30
00:04:36.800 --> 00:04:47.840
[George] He urged farmers to practice systematic crop rotation, alternating cotton with leguminous nitrogen fixing crops, peanuts, cowpeas, velvet beans and sweet potatoes.

31
00:04:47.840 --> 00:04:58.960
[George] Legume roots harbored symbiotic bacteria that pulled nitrogen from the air and deposited it directly into the soil, restoring fertility without expensive chemical fertilizers.

32
00:04:58.960 --> 00:05:14.320
[George] When the catastrophic bull weevil insect plagues swept across the South in the 1910s, devastating cotton crops and threatening mass famine, Carver's diversification gospels saved thousands of black and white farmers from total economic ruin.

33
00:05:14.320 --> 00:05:29.200
[George] Carver analyzed the soil chemistry of the South with microscopic precision, demonstrating that continuous cotton cultivation functioned as a toxic extractive industry that depleted the soil of vital nitrates, phosphates, and organic humus.

34
00:05:29.200 --> 00:05:33.880
[George] Leaving hard red clay gullies that could no longer retain rainwater.

35
00:05:33.880 --> 00:05:52.920
[George] Carver preached the biological gospel of leguminous crop rotation, crops like the peanut, the cowpea, and the sweet potato had root nodules housing rhizobium bacteria that pulled nitrogen gas out of the atmosphere and fixed it directly into the soil, creating a natural, self-renewing fertilizer.

36
00:05:52.920 --> 00:06:09.080
[George] When the devastating bull weevil beetle crossed the Rio Grande and decimated cotton crops across the Deep South, farmers who had followed Carver's advice were spared financial catastrophe, having diversified their acreage into lucrative crops that insects could not destroy.

37
00:06:09.080 --> 00:06:27.000
[George] Recognizing that impoverished sharecroppers could not afford to travel to Tuskegee for lectures, Carver designed the Jessup Agricultural Wagon in 1906, a horse-drawn demonstration wagon equipped with soil testing kits, butter churns, fertilizer samples, and agricultural charts.

38
00:06:27.000 --> 00:06:42.280
[George] Traveling into the poorest rural backcountry of Alabama, Carver held outdoor workshops under pine trees, teaching illiterate farmers how to compost swamp muck, cultivate home vegetable gardens, raise livestock, and improve sanitation.

39
00:06:42.280 --> 00:07:01.560
[George] He published 44 practical, plain language agricultural research bulletins, distributing them for free across the South with titles like, How to Grow the Peanut and 105 Ways of Preparing It for Human Consumption, teaching farm families how to feed their children nutritious diets on pennies a day.

40
00:07:01.560 --> 00:07:16.480
[George] Carver designed the Jessup Agricultural Wagon as a mobile university on wheels, outfitting a heavy wooden farm wagon with soil samples, grafting knives, seed separators, and canning equipment financed by New York philanthropist Morris K.

41
00:07:14.840 --> 00:07:17.040
[George] Jessup.

42
00:07:17.440 --> 00:07:33.760
[George] Riding through the piny woods on dirt roads, Carver held evening demonstrations in churchyards and cotton fields, showing illiterate sharecroppers how to compost decaying oak leaves, make paint from colored Alabama clay, and preserve wild berries in glass jars.

43
00:07:33.760 --> 00:07:51.200
[George] His simple, practical agricultural bulletins were written in warm, conversational prose, explaining complex biological processes in everyday terms and teaching rural farm families how to achieve self-sufficiency, health, and economic independence from predatory merchant lenders.

44
00:07:51.200 --> 00:07:57.040
[George] As southern farmers embraced crop rotation, an unexpected economic crisis arose.

45
00:07:57.040 --> 00:08:04.720
[George] Farmers produced millions of pounds of peanuts and sweet potatoes, but there was no commercial market to absorb the massive surplus.

46
00:08:04.720 --> 00:08:20.800
[George] Retreating into his Tuskegee laboratory at 4 o'clock every morning, praying to God for guidance to unravel the secrets of the plant, Carver used chemical fractionation to break the peanut down into its constituent fats, oils, sugars, starches and proteins.

47
00:08:20.800 --> 00:08:36.320
[George] From the humble peanut, Carver developed over 300 distinct commercial products milk, cheese, instant coffee, cooking oils, flour, chili sauce, wood stains, face creams, axle grease, paper, and synthetic rubber.

48
00:08:36.320 --> 00:08:49.760
[George] From the sweet potato, he created 118 products, including postage stamp glue, vinegar, flour and molasses, transforming lowly southern crops into industrial raw materials.

49
00:08:49.760 --> 00:08:54.960
[George] Carver's chemical laboratory in Tuskegee was a modern crucible of alchemical wonder.

50
00:08:54.960 --> 00:09:04.760
[George] Waking before dawn to walk through the woods and pray for divine guidance, Carver returned to his lab to systematically deconstruct the molecular architecture of the peanut.

51
00:09:04.760 --> 00:09:21.880
[George] From the peanut's rich vegetable oils and proteins, Carver synthesized 30 different types of milk, cheeses, cooking oils, flour, synthetic rubber, wood stains, axle grease, insulating board, and medicinal massage oils that relieved pain in polio victims.

52
00:09:21.880 --> 00:09:38.920
[George] From the sweet potato, he created 118 products, including starch, vinegar, molasses and synthetic textiles, demonstrating that the renewable agricultural harvest of the soil could replace nonrenewable fossil fuels as industrial raw materials.

53
00:09:38.920 --> 00:09:49.960
[George] In January 1921, Carver appeared before the United States House Ways and Means Committee in Washington to testify in support of a tariff on foreign peanut imports.

54
00:09:49.960 --> 00:10:02.920
[George] Granted a scant ten minutes by skeptical, condescending congressmen, Carver placed his jars and samples on the table, speaking with humorous, luminous brilliance about the infinite possibilities of the peanut.

55
00:10:02.920 --> 00:10:15.400
[George] Mesmerized by his presentation, the committee repeatedly extended his time, culminating in a thunderous standing ovation and the passage of the landmark Fordney McCumber Tariff of 1922.

56
00:10:15.400 --> 00:10:26.680
[George] Despite offers of hundred thousand dollar salaries from industrial titans like Thomas Edison and Henry Ford, Carver chose to remain at Tuskegee, refusing to patent his inventions.

57
00:10:26.680 --> 00:10:30.200
[George] God gave them to me, how can I sell them to someone else?

58
00:10:30.200 --> 00:10:32.920
[George] He lived in a simple dormitory room.

59
00:10:32.920 --> 00:10:41.240
[George] Wore clothes he mendied himself, and donated his life savings of $60,000 to establish the Carver Research Foundation.

60
00:10:41.240 --> 00:10:53.600
[George] Carver's historic appearance before the House Ways and Means Committee in 1921 was an extraordinary masterclass in disarming racial prejudice with pure intellectual brilliance and magnetic charm.

61
00:10:53.600 --> 00:11:11.760
[George] When Carver stepped to the podium, condescending Southern congressmen insulted him with racial slurs and gave him only ten minutes to speak, but as Carver unpacked jars of peanut coffee, peanut milk, and peanut dyes, explaining their industrial value with witty, brilliant humor.

62
00:11:11.760 --> 00:11:20.240
[George] The congressmen were utterly spellbound, repeatedly extending his speaking time and passing the tariff that saved the southern peanut industry.

63
00:11:20.240 --> 00:11:25.360
[George] Carver's refusal to patent his discoveries was rooted in his saintly Christian faith.

64
00:11:25.360 --> 00:11:38.320
[George] He believed that God's creations were free gifts to humanity, repeatedly refusing lucrative industrial contracts from Henry Ford and Thomas Edison to remain at Tuskegee serving impoverished rural farmers.

65
00:11:38.320 --> 00:11:55.040
[George] In his final years, Carver was revered as an international sage of humanitarian science, corresponding with Indian independence leader Mahatma Gandhi about vegetarian nutrition and hosting close friend Henry Ford at Tuskegee to develop peanut based plastics for automobiles.

66
00:11:55.040 --> 00:12:02.000
[George] Passing away on January 5, 1943, at age 78, President Franklin D.

67
00:12:02.000 --> 00:12:11.360
[George] Roosevelt signed legislation dedicating a national monument in his honor, the first national monument dedicated to an African American in United States history.

68
00:12:11.360 --> 00:12:16.960
[George] His tombstone at Tuskegee bears an epitaph that captures the sublime nobility of his life.

69
00:12:16.960 --> 00:12:20.880
[George] He could have added fortune to fame, but caring for neither.

70
00:12:20.880 --> 00:12:24.560
[George] He found happiness and honor in being helpful to the world.

71
00:12:24.560 --> 00:12:34.600
[George] George Washington Carver remains an eternal symbol of scientific humility, proving that the greatest discoveries are those made in selfless service to the earth and mankind.

72
00:12:34.600 --> 00:12:48.920
[George] In his later years, Carver's humble laboratory in Alabama became a global destination for world leaders, scientists, and philanthropists, who marveled at his gentle spirit, botanical genius, and unshakable inner peace.

73
00:12:48.920 --> 00:13:01.080
[George] When Mahatma Gandhi sought advice on developing nutritious vegetarian diets for India's impoverished masses, Carver sent him detailed botanical guides on utilizing local legumes and soy crops.

74
00:13:01.080 --> 00:13:10.200
[George] George Washington Carver passed away peacefully in January 1943, leaving behind an immortal legacy that transcended the color line.

75
00:13:10.200 --> 00:13:23.160
[George] He had healed the depleted soil of the South, delivered millions of poor farmers from economic bondage, and proved that a gentle, humble soul walking hand in hand with nature could bless the entire human race.

76
00:13:23.800 --> 00:13:26.840
[Rebecca] Thank you for listening to most fascinating stories.

77
00:13:26.840 --> 00:13:36.840
[Rebecca] George Washington Carver's selfless botanical brilliance, deep spiritual humility, and love for the earth healed generations of farmers across the American South.

78
00:13:36.840 --> 00:13:45.960
[Rebecca] Join us next week as Rebecca returns with the grand finale of our top 50 American series, John Muir and the Birth of the American Conservation Movement.

79
00:13:45.960 --> 00:13:55.800
[Rebecca] Until then, stay curious.

